fuse: don't flush times of unlinked inodes to the server

With writeback_cache the client owns file timestamps: unlink dirties the
victim's ctime (fuse_entry_unlinked -> fuse_update_ctime_in_cache ->
mark_inode_dirty_sync), and the dirty inode is later flushed through
fuse_write_inode -> fuse_flush_times -> a synchronous SETATTR round trip
to the server -- for a file that no longer has any path. Nothing can
observe those timestamps except fstat() through a still-open fd of the
unlinked file, which may now see a stale ctime: the same fudge network
filesystems make routinely.

Measured on virtkit's virtio-fs (20k-file create/unlink storm per
variant, server-side syscall counts): writeback_cache went from 0 to
100k utimensat calls for 60k unlinked files, and unlink-heavy phases
ran 30-40% slower than with writeback off. With this patch the
dead-inode SETATTRs disappear and unlink cost returns to the
non-writeback baseline, keeping writeback's multi-write win (~4x).

Hardlinked files keep the flush: the skip triggers only once i_nlink
reaches 0.

Candidate for upstream submission; carried here until then. Re-check
applicability on every kernel bump (fs/fuse/file.c, fuse_write_inode).

--- a/fs/fuse/file.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/file.c
@@ -2002,6 +2002,14 @@ int fuse_write_inode(struct inode *inode, struct writeback_control *wbc)
 	struct fuse_file *ff;
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * The timestamps of an unlinked inode are observable only through a
+	 * still-open fd; don't pay a synchronous SETATTR round trip to the
+	 * server for them (writeback_cache dirties ctime on every unlink).
+	 */
+	if (inode->i_nlink == 0)
+		return 0;
+
 	ff = __fuse_write_file_get(fi);
 	err = fuse_flush_times(inode, ff);
 	if (ff)
